A1. It did no good to say "out — damp spot" to this. (Init.) ~ USA
A4. Love makes 'em get this way. ~ DAZED
A8. A sand hill. ~ TEE
A11. This is a little doggie. ~ SPANIEL
A13. These are the very latest things. ~ MODERNS
A16. This is supposed to be a fast time. ~ LENT
A17. What was the man who said, "Two can live cheaper than one"? ~ LIAR
A18. The shoemaker who sticks to his last should use this. ~ AWL
A20. Sometimes used to influence a jury. ~ SOB
A22. What a man becomes if he lives a long time in Sweden. ~ OLD
A23. A poetic form of frequently. ~ OFT
A25. A little girl's exclamation. ~ OOH
A27. Often used to describe Horizontal 13. ~ ULTRA
A28. A sign of dramatic success. ~ SRO
A29. This is often found hanging around rich widows. ~ JEWELRY
A31. This often raises a kick. ~ RAISIN
A34. A pronoun. ~ US
A35. An old-fashioned girl become modern. ~ MA
A36. A proposition, essay or dissertation. ~ THESIS
A39. An exclamation. ~ LO
A40. This sits all over the younger generation. ~ HEN
A41. The motorist's favorite sign. ~ GO
A43. An obsolete kind of parent. ~ SEVERE
A45. Everyone taken separately. (Abbr.) ~ EA
A47. Indefinite article. ~ AN
A48. A dear skin. ~ ERMINE
A50. These should give you a good steer. ~ RUDDERS
A53. A wide open space. ~ GAP
A54. Some people make these go up in smoke. ~ RINGS
A56. This is a cut-up. ~ AXE
A57. Swine—as Mrs. Smyth spells it. ~ PYG
A58. Silent salesmen. (Abbr.) ~ ADS
A59. Conjunction. ~ YET
A61. The girl friend who asks if you aren't getting stouter. ~ CAT
A63. A Hindu philosopher. ~ YOGI
A65. Half of feminism. ~ FEMI
A67. Abie's Irish Rose is full of this. ~ DIALECT
A70. If this applies to you, you must be crazy. ~ LUNATIC
A72. Before. ~ ERE
A73. Anyone can get this if they figure correctly. ~ TOTAL
A74. This often gets lost at the races. ~ BET

D1. An adverb applying to something above. ~ UP
D2. What is a paleface? ~ SALLOW
D3. The Scotchman begins counting. ~ ANE
D4. What most people do who start out on the straight and narrow road. ~ DETOUR
D5. We all know this man. ~ AL
D6. The printer's contribution to cross words. ~ EM
D7. What makes a capitalist out of a socialist? ~ DOLLARS
D8. You get this at English filling-stations. ~ TEA
D9. These are deadly in the medical profession. ~ ERRORS
D10. The printer makes another contribution. ~ EN
D11. This is deep stuff. ~ SEA
D12. These get all the plums in politics. ~ INS
D14. Accomplished. ~ DID
D15. The old soak. ~ SOT
D19. Troubles. ~ WOES
D21. How the third cocktail makes you feel. ~ BLYTHE
D22. Conjunction. ~ OR
D24. What you like to see the hero do to the villain. ~ FOIL
D26. The wavering line in women's dress. ~ HEM
D28. Little brother's nemesis. ~ SIS
D29. This is often taken for a joke. ~ JUDGE
D30. The way the commuter comes home. ~ LADEN
D32. We don't want the family skeleton to be this. ~ AIRED
D33. These come every day. ~ NOONS
D37. Pronoun. ~ HER
D38. It takes this to get on top. ~ ENERGY
D42. A wild old party. ~ ORGY
D43. Just a little drink. ~ SIP
D44. A decision. ~ VERDICT
D46. Girl's name. ~ ADA
D47. This is surface stuff. ~ AREA
D49. A race predominant in Hungary. ~ MAGYAR
D51. What an alibi should be. ~ USEFUL
D52. What a pink elephant would do to Vertical 15. ~ EXCITE
D55. Part of the verb "to be." ~ IS
D57. Close quarters for the old bean. ~ POD
D58. There's no telling this in the sewing circle. ~ AGE
D60. A number. ~ TEN
D62. A twitching. ~ TIC
D64. This is old to a darky. ~ OLE
D66. A fairy queen. ~ MAB
D68. That is. (Abbr.) ~ IE
D69. Preposition. ~ TO
D70. Note of the scale. ~ LA
D71. That inexpressible charm. ~ IT


Instructions:
Scratch Pads of Famous Men do! SOLOMON

